   "text": "# Lecture 2 — Cellular respiration\n\nCell Biology (demo course) · Week 2 · my notes from the lecture\n\n## Overview\n\nCellular respiration releases the energy stored in glucose and stores it as ATP.\nOverall: C6H12O6 + 6 O2 → 6 CO2 + 6 H2O + energy (about 30–32 ATP per glucose).\n\nFour stages, in this order:\n\n1. Glycolysis — cytoplasm\n2. Link reaction (pyruvate oxidation) — mitochondrial matrix\n3. Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) — mitochondrial matrix\n4. Electron transport chain + oxidative phosphorylation — inner mitochondrial membrane\n\n## 1. Glycolysis\n\n- Glucose (6C) is split into 2 pyruvate (3C).\n- Does not need oxygen.\n- Uses 2 ATP, makes 4 ATP → net gain **2 ATP**, plus **2 NADH**.\n\n## 2. Link reaction\n\n- Each pyruvate enters the mitochondrion and becomes acetyl-CoA (2C).\n- Releases 1 CO2 and makes 1 NADH per pyruvate (so 2 CO2 + 2 NADH per glucose).\n\n## 3. Krebs cycle\n\n- Acetyl-CoA (2C) joins oxaloacetate (4C) to form citrate (6C); the cycle regenerates oxaloacetate.\n- Per turn: **3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 ATP (or GTP), 2 CO2**.\n- The cycle turns **twice per glucose** (one turn per acetyl-CoA) — lecturer said this is the most common\n  exam trap: double the per-turn numbers when asked \"per glucose\".\n\n## 4. Electron transport chain (ETC)\n\n- NADH and FADH2 hand their electrons to protein complexes in the inner membrane.\n- The energy pumps H+ ions into the intermembrane space → a proton gradient.\n- H+ flows back through **ATP synthase**, which makes most of the ATP (chemiosmosis).\n- **Oxygen is the final electron acceptor** and forms water. Without O2 the chain stops.\n\n## Without oxygen: fermentation\n\n- Only glycolysis runs, so only 2 ATP per glucose.\n- Fermentation regenerates NAD+ so glycolysis can continue:\n  - animals: pyruvate → lactate (lactic acid fermentation, e.g. muscles during a sprint)\n  - yeast: pyruvate → ethanol + CO2 (alcoholic fermentation)\n\n## Lecturer's summary\n\nATP per glucose: glycolysis 2 + Krebs 2 + oxidative phosphorylation ~26–28 ≈ 30–32 in total."
