   "text": "Lecture 1 — Membrane transport\nCell Biology (demo course) · Week 1\n\n\n1.1 The plasma membrane\n\nThe plasma membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins (the fluid\nmosaic model). Its hydrophobic core lets small non-polar molecules (O2, CO2) pass,\nbut blocks ions and large polar molecules such as glucose. The membrane is\ntherefore selectively permeable.\n\n1.2 Passive transport\n\nPassive transport needs no energy from the cell: substances move down their\nconcentration gradient, from high to low concentration, until equilibrium.\n\n      Simple diffusion — directly through the bilayer (O2, CO2, steroid hormones).\n      Facilitated diffusion — through channel proteins or carrier proteins (glucose\n      via GLUT transporters, ions via ion channels). Still passive: no ATP is used.\n\nThe rate of diffusion increases with a steeper gradient, higher temperature and a\nlarger surface area, and decreases with a longer diffusion distance."
