   "text": "1.4 Active transport\n\nActive transport moves substances against their concentration gradient, using\nenergy, usually from ATP. It needs specific carrier proteins called pumps.\n\nKey example — the sodium–potassium pump (Na+/K+-ATPase): for each ATP\nhydrolysed it moves 3 Na+ out of the cell and 2 K+ in. This keeps the inside of the\ncell negative and maintains the resting membrane potential of neurons.\n\n1.5 Bulk transport\n\n     Endocytosis — the membrane folds inwards to take material in: phagocytosis\n     (\"cell eating\", e.g. a white blood cell engulfing bacteria) and pinocytosis (\"cell\n     drinking\").\n     Exocytosis — vesicles fuse with the membrane to release their contents (e.g.\n     neurotransmitters, hormones).\n\nBoth use ATP, so they count as active processes.\n\nSummary\n\nPassive (diffusion, facilitated diffusion, osmosis): down the gradient, no ATP. Active\n(pumps, endo- and exocytosis): against the gradient or in bulk, needs ATP."
